<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiankang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">229–589</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Jianye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiankang was the capital of the southern Chinese dynasties on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. Founded as Jianye, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a>'s state of Eastern Wu from 229, it was renamed Jiankang in 313 and served as the seat of the Eastern Jin and of the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Southern Qi, Liang and Chen dynasties until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> conquest of 589. It grew into one of the largest cities in the world and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and aristocratic culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiankang.jpg" alt="Jiankang" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: &quot;Jinling Ancient and Modern Map Research&quot; written by Chen Yi in the Ming Dynasty. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Southern_Dynasties_map_of_Nanjing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
